Okay, so replies to this thread seem to fall under a few main points. In 
addition, I don't see anyone actually wanting something like this, so I 
might as well save myself the time and not make it. After replying to a 
few posts separately, I've decided to just address them all here.


  Point one: It's too hard to implement.

As someone with a fair amount of programming experience, I know that 
it's not too hard to implement. For instance, isometric projection at 
the angle mentioned is simply a matter of drawing a tile at, say, (xc*64 
+ yc*8, yc*40), where xc is the x-coordinate of the tile and yc is the 
y-coordinate. In fact, it's already done in GTK+ Hack and 
Falcon's/Vulture's Eye/Claw.


  Point two: No one wants it.

On the contrary, I know many people who would want it. I mean, sure, 
there are people who like to imagine things for themselves, and they can 
go play in ASCII mode, but there's a reason why the tiled interface 
exists. And noeGNUd, GTK+ Hack, and Falcon's Eye, for that matter.


  Point three: People still play chess, and /that/ doesn't have good
    graphics.

And people still play [insert random game with good graphics here], as 
well. Different people like different things. Some people like gameplay 
more than graphics, some people like graphics more than gameplay. Some 
people like gameplay, but would like it even better if it came with good 
graphics *gasp*.


  Point four: OMG! BUT I LIKE THE ASCII INTERFACE! WHY THE HECK ARE YOU
    TAKING IT OUT??!!!!!oneoneone

Read my post again. I didn't mention getting rid of the ASCII interface. 
An ASCII interface and a graphical interface doesn't have to be mutually 
exclusive. It's not like creating a graphical interface will cause 
people in black suits to force their way into your house, put a gun to 
your head, and threaten to shoot you if you don't delete the ASCII 
interface and use the graphical interface.


  Point five: [insert nitpick here]

That's a good point. But that doesn't mean it's not a good idea, it 
simply means it's not perfect and could be refined, just like every 
other idea in existence.


I think those are the only points being made.

